"# aos-directive: resolve directives\n\n```sh\necho '{\"p\": {\"$env\": \"HOME\", \"$path\": \"/x\"}, \"r\": {\"$ref\": \"other.json#/x\"}}' | aos-directive\n# {\"p\": \"/home/me/x\", \"r\": {\"y\": \"hi\"}}\n```\n\nIt reads one JSON document from stdin (empty = `{}`), resolves every directive in it (`$ref`, `$env`, `$path`, ...; relative paths from the current directory; `./` and `../` work) and prints the result as one line. A `$ref` path follows one rule: left of `#` is a file path counted from the folder of the file that holds the `$ref` (`./`, `../`, absolute; at the top, from the folder of `--file FILE`, or the current directory without it); right of `#` is inside the JSON. `{\"$ref\": \"x.json#/a/b\"}` is `/a/b` of x.json; `#/a/b` (no file name) is `/a/b` from the root of the file that holds the `$ref` (at the top the file given by `--file FILE`; without `--file` it is left as it is); `#./b` counts from the object that holds the `$ref`, `#../c` from one level above that (repeatable; above the root is `DirectiveBad`); `#` or `#/` is the whole file. `#aaa`, `y.json#aaa`, `y.json#./b` and `y.json#../b` are `DirectiveBad`. An unset variable or an unreadable `$ref` = `DirectiveBad`; stdin that is not JSON = `InputBad`. Other aos programs (aos-exec, the clock register, tool registries) resolve the same directives themselves; use this to see what a config will turn into.\n"
